00:00:59.732 --> 00:01:00.662
Uh, John became the.

00:01:01.537 --> 00:01:05.017
Whistleblower indicted by the Obama
administration under the Espionage

00:01:05.017 --> 00:01:07.717
Act, a law designed to punish spies.

00:01:08.167 --> 00:01:12.397
He served 23 months in prison as the
result of his attempts to oppose the

00:01:12.397 --> 00:01:14.017
Bush Administration's Torture program.

00:01:14.407 --> 00:01:16.297
John, welcome to Fortress On A Hill.

00:01:16.802 --> 00:01:17.742
John Kirakou: Thanks for having me.

00:01:18.962 --> 00:01:22.772
Henri: So I, I'd, um, I'd like to
give the listeners kind of a sense

00:01:22.802 --> 00:01:26.552
of, um, your, your overall background.

00:01:26.552 --> 00:01:30.242
Certainly we don't have to go in
everything into, into great detail, but

00:01:30.247 --> 00:01:34.802
I'd like to, to hear a bit about your
career, about, um, how you came to the

00:01:34.802 --> 00:01:41.612
point that you became a, a whistleblower
and, um, what, how you see that

00:01:41.612 --> 00:01:43.232
career now, today, looking back on it.

00:01:44.537 --> 00:01:45.227
John Kirakou: Sure.

00:01:45.557 --> 00:01:48.587
The second part of the, uh, of the
question is the more difficult one.

00:01:48.617 --> 00:01:53.477
The, the easier part is I was recruited
into the CIA when I was in graduate school

00:01:53.687 --> 00:01:55.787
at, uh, George Washington University.

00:01:56.642 --> 00:02:04.202
I, uh, I had an advisor, a professor whom
I respected very deeply, uh, and, and I

00:02:04.262 --> 00:02:06.602
continued to respect until, until he died.

00:02:07.052 --> 00:02:07.742
Uh, that was Dr.

00:02:07.742 --> 00:02:08.432
Gerald Post.

00:02:08.437 --> 00:02:12.092
He recruited me into the cia
my second year in grad school.

00:02:12.872 --> 00:02:16.292
And, um, I started at the c i
A the first week of January,

00:02:16.752 --> 00:02:19.562
1990 as an analyst on Iraq.

00:02:19.832 --> 00:02:27.197
And, um, Uh, spent the first seven
and a half years of my career working

00:02:27.257 --> 00:02:31.547
exclusively on Iraq, including a
tour overseas in the Middle East,

00:02:31.787 --> 00:02:33.347
working Iraqi sanctions issues.

00:02:34.337 --> 00:02:37.697
I was in, uh, an office at
the CIA that no longer exists.

00:02:37.697 --> 00:02:40.427
It's called, it was called the
Office of Leadership Analysis.

00:02:40.907 --> 00:02:45.527
So my job really was to be Saddam
Hussein's classified biographer, or his

00:02:45.527 --> 00:02:47.447
biographer for the intelligence community.

00:02:47.747 --> 00:02:51.797
I got bored with that after seven,
seven and a half years, and so

00:02:51.802 --> 00:02:56.387
I made a very unusual switch to
counter-terrorism operations.

00:02:56.957 --> 00:03:02.387
Because at the time, um, I was the
only person, quite literally, I was

00:03:02.387 --> 00:03:06.107
the only person in the c I A who
was fluent in both Greek and Arabic.

00:03:06.962 --> 00:03:12.692
So, uh, they sent me to Athens as the,
um, counter-terrorism representative,

00:03:13.382 --> 00:03:21.662
uh, mostly covering Arab Terrorism Abu
Organization, P F L P D F L P P F L P G

00:03:21.662 --> 00:03:27.132
C, all those old school communist, uh, and
nationalist terrorist groups, and a Greek

00:03:27.362 --> 00:03:29.222
group called Revolutionary Organization.

00:03:29.662 --> 00:03:29.862
17.

00:03:31.412 --> 00:03:36.962
I did that until, uh, the summer of
2000 and then went back to headquarters.

00:03:37.022 --> 00:03:43.622
I was, I was, um, working, um, I
have to be careful of my language.

00:03:43.627 --> 00:03:47.162
I was, I was training Middle
Eastern Intelligence services and

00:03:47.167 --> 00:03:53.792
counter-terrorism operations and then
nine 11 hit and, uh, I was named,

00:03:53.792 --> 00:03:58.922
um, the chief of Counter-Terrorism
Operations in Pakistan after nine.

00:04:00.437 --> 00:04:06.287
Um, in that job I led a series of
raids that resulted in the capture of

00:04:06.287 --> 00:04:10.547
Abu Zubahdah, who we believed at the
time was the number three in Al Qaeda.

00:04:10.607 --> 00:04:11.297
He wasn't.

00:04:12.077 --> 00:04:14.417
And, um, went back to headquarters.

00:04:14.422 --> 00:04:17.507
On the strength of the Abu
Zubahdah capture, I got promoted

00:04:17.507 --> 00:04:23.087
to, uh, executive assistant to
the CIA's deputy director and.

00:04:25.502 --> 00:04:29.222
And then my final tour was at the
United Nations in New York, and I

00:04:29.222 --> 00:04:32.672
resigned from, from New York to go
into the private Sector and then

00:04:32.672 --> 00:04:36.782
later back into government at the uh,
Senate Foreign Relations Committee.

00:04:37.732 --> 00:04:42.682
Henri: Um, I'm curious about your time
working and, and again, I, like you

00:04:42.682 --> 00:04:47.182
said, I'm real careful about what you,
what you can share, but the time working

00:04:47.182 --> 00:04:53.587
on Iraqi sanctions, um, uh, I'm curious
about, especially about that time

00:04:53.592 --> 00:05:00.127
because it, it, you know, it in some
ways it informs the state of Iraq today

00:05:00.637 --> 00:05:02.677
almost as much as the Iraq war does.

00:05:03.277 --> 00:05:06.277
Um, but is there any, is there
anything that you can share

00:05:06.282 --> 00:05:07.687
with us about, about that time?

00:05:07.692 --> 00:05:11.147
Just kind of the, you know, the,
maybe your overall impressions of it?

00:05:12.382 --> 00:05:15.532
John Kirakou: Yeah, it was, it
was that long period of Iraqi

00:05:15.982 --> 00:05:17.182
sanctions that convinced me.

00:05:18.667 --> 00:05:20.497
Number one, sanctions don't work.

00:05:21.487 --> 00:05:28.537
And number two, they are cruel
to the point of becoming, in many

00:05:28.537 --> 00:05:31.477
cases, crimes against humanity.

00:05:32.347 --> 00:05:35.167
You know, I remember in the very
beginning of the sanctions regime,

00:05:35.167 --> 00:05:42.727
beginning in August of 1990, um, the
State Department of the White House took

00:05:42.727 --> 00:05:47.107
very seriously the notion that the Iraqi
people needed to continue receiving.

00:05:47.707 --> 00:05:48.747
Food and medicine.

00:05:49.177 --> 00:05:55.717
Okay, so, so they, they wrote up a
UN security council resolution 9 86,

00:05:56.587 --> 00:06:02.407
and then the Iraqi said, well, we
need, you know, these pipes, right?

00:06:02.437 --> 00:06:04.087
For our sewer system.

00:06:04.657 --> 00:06:06.597
We can't deliver clean
water without these pipes.

00:06:06.597 --> 00:06:09.217
Well, then the CIA says, wait a minute.

00:06:09.877 --> 00:06:15.667
Those kinds of pipes are used in South
Africa to run fiber optic cable, and

00:06:15.667 --> 00:06:20.197
you can use fiber optic cable for a
weapons of mass destruction program.

00:06:20.197 --> 00:06:21.067
So no pipes.

00:06:21.997 --> 00:06:23.287
Well with no pipes.

00:06:23.407 --> 00:06:27.007
Then you get no clean water and
when you get no clean water,

00:06:27.012 --> 00:06:28.327
people start to get sick.

00:06:28.747 --> 00:06:33.487
And then, The US government says,
well, you know, they shouldn't be using

00:06:33.487 --> 00:06:36.427
this much, uh, in, uh, antibiotics.

00:06:36.427 --> 00:06:38.617
They must be doing something
with the, an antibiotics.

00:06:38.617 --> 00:06:40.237
They're making a BW program out of it.

00:06:41.497 --> 00:06:47.047
And the next thing you know, thousands,
tens of thousands of people, mostly

00:06:47.047 --> 00:06:52.177
children, are dying and they're dying
as a direct result of these sanctions.

00:06:53.287 --> 00:06:57.757
And I remember a senior officer
telling me, sanctions are always a bad.

00:06:58.957 --> 00:07:02.737
Because they never do anything
to the person being targeted.

00:07:03.067 --> 00:07:05.187
Saddam Hussein didn't give
a shit about sanction.

00:07:06.817 --> 00:07:10.567
He had a very sophisticated
sanctions busting regime set up at

00:07:10.567 --> 00:07:14.167
the American em, or at the Iraqi
Embassy in, uh, Amman, Jordan.

00:07:14.797 --> 00:07:18.577
He had a, a cousin who was in
charge of it at the time, and

00:07:18.577 --> 00:07:21.337
so it was only the Iraqi people.

00:07:21.667 --> 00:07:25.597
And when I say the Iraqi people, I
mean the innocence among the Iraqi

00:07:25.597 --> 00:07:30.397
people, women, children, minorities,
you know, Kurds, Shia, they were

00:07:30.397 --> 00:07:31.687
the ones who were suffering.

00:07:32.257 --> 00:07:40.507
It wasn't Saddam or his Trei clan or
his Aja family, or really any Sunnis

00:07:40.507 --> 00:07:44.047
in the, in the power structure of
the Iraqi government and military.

00:07:44.557 --> 00:07:47.467
None of them suffered
at all from sanctions.

00:07:47.827 --> 00:07:49.497
The Iraqi people suffered from sanction.

00:07:50.602 --> 00:07:53.392
Uh, you know, we renewed
sanctions so many times.

00:07:53.542 --> 00:07:59.002
Uh, uh, after 9 86, it was 10 96,
and then it was like 1234, and it

00:07:59.007 --> 00:08:00.652
was just year after year after year.

00:08:00.657 --> 00:08:03.232
Sanctions were being, were being renewed.

00:08:03.442 --> 00:08:06.592
And I remember saying to a colleague
of mine, a buddy of mine at the

00:08:06.592 --> 00:08:11.422
time, I can't do this as a career.

00:08:12.682 --> 00:08:13.972
Nothing's gonna change.

00:08:14.212 --> 00:08:19.132
American policy's not gonna change, and
we're not serious about taking out Saddam,

00:08:19.252 --> 00:08:23.242
nor should we have been because that in
and of itself would've been a violation

00:08:23.247 --> 00:08:25.702
of international law as it was later.

00:08:26.272 --> 00:08:30.112
Um, but I just decided I, I had to do
something different and more interesting.

00:08:30.142 --> 00:08:34.072
And every, let me tell you, every Greek
American at the cia, all through the

00:08:34.072 --> 00:08:37.002
CIA's history has wanted to serve in Athe.

00:08:38.122 --> 00:08:42.277
And so that's what we all sought to do
and I ended up getting a great tour there.

00:08:43.277 --> 00:08:47.707
Henri: I remember speaking to, um,
I did, I did two tours in Iraq as

00:08:47.707 --> 00:08:52.447
a, as an Army mp, and I remember
speaking to, Different people.

00:08:52.447 --> 00:08:55.387
It was usually tra uh, the translators
that we had cuz it was easier

00:08:55.387 --> 00:08:57.037
for us to, to go back and forth.

00:08:57.517 --> 00:09:01.657
But talking about just that, that
entire period and both, you know, the

00:09:01.657 --> 00:09:07.147
Gulf War in and of itself, the, um,
the massive killings that happened

00:09:07.147 --> 00:09:09.127
to those who rose up against Saddam.

00:09:09.487 --> 00:09:11.017
Following George Bush's call.

00:09:11.287 --> 00:09:14.107
And that was one that, this, this
particular guy I'm thinking of

00:09:14.107 --> 00:09:15.517
that, that was a huge thing for him.

00:09:15.907 --> 00:09:20.857
But then, and then, you know, children,
dead children, you know, from no food,

00:09:20.857 --> 00:09:26.647
from no medicine, but in terms of trying
to understand, you know, the, the, the

00:09:26.677 --> 00:09:31.897
